{% trace-scenario title="Platform choice: feature fit or cost trap?" prompt="Your team is choosing between LangSmith and Uncoil for agent observability. Both can capture traces, but LangSmith charges $2.50 per 1,000 traces after a monthly quota. At what volume does this stop being a rounding error and start being the deciding factor?" outcome="Calculate your true 12-month cost of ownership. Below roughly 1M traces/month the two are close. Past a few million, LangSmith's overage line and Uncoil's flat line stop tracking each other, and the gap compounds every month you wait to switch." /%}

## At a glance

![Line chart showing one team's monthly observability bill over 12 months as trace volume grows from 5M to 120M per month: LangSmith rises from $10,217 to $277,490 while Uncoil stays flat at $300 before stepping to $1,500](/media/langsmith-vs-uncoil-growth-trajectory.svg)

You are evaluating observability platforms for multi-agent production systems. Both LangSmith and Uncoil capture traces, support evaluations, and integrate with your stack. The features overlap more than either vendor's marketing admits. The difference that actually determines your bill is the pricing model — specifically, what happens to cost as trace volume grows past your plan's included quota.

**LangSmith** charges per seat plus a base fee, with a trace quota included in each plan. Traces beyond that quota are billed at $2.50 per 1,000. **Uncoil** charges a flat monthly tier with no per-trace metering and no quota gate. Below roughly a million traces per month, most teams sit inside or near their LangSmith quota and the two platforms cost about the same. Once overage kicks in, the two cost curves stop being comparable at all — by the time you reach 100M–200M monthly traces, LangSmith can run 100 to 300 times more than Uncoil's equivalent tier.

This article works through that math at four concrete volume tiers, states LangSmith's genuine strengths without hedging, and gives you a decision framework and migration checklist so the choice is deliberate rather than accidental.

{% highlight %}The quota gate is a pricing decision, not a technical requirement — LangSmith's infrastructure and Uncoil's infrastructure can both capture every trace you send them. The only question is which one charges you $2.50 per 1,000 for the privilege past a fixed line, and which one doesn't have that line at all.{% /highlight %}

### The operating model: quota-based vs. all-in-one pricing

**LangSmith's model:** a base fee plus per-seat pricing, with a trace quota bundled into each plan (larger on Enterprise, smaller on Growth). Traces beyond the quota are billed at $2.50 per 1,000 — a rate that can be discounted 20-30% for committed-volume Enterprise contracts, but never eliminated. This model rewards teams who keep volume near or under quota and penalizes teams who don't, which quietly pushes architecture decisions toward sampling.

**Uncoil's model:** a single monthly tier (Pro, Enterprise) with a generous included-events ceiling and no per-trace overage line item at all. Cost moves with plan tier — features, seats, evaluation runs — not with how many traces you actually ingest. This model rewards comprehensive observation because there's no meter counting against you.

| Dimension | LangSmith | Uncoil |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Pricing basis** | Per-seat + base + per-trace overage | Flat monthly tier |
| **Trace quota** | 1M/mo (Growth-tier assumption) to 10M/mo (negotiated Enterprise) | No per-trace quota |
| **Overage cost** | $2.50 / 1K traces (20-30% discount possible at Enterprise) | None |
| **Cost predictability** | Variable — depends on actual monthly volume | Fixed — set by plan tier |
| **Architectural pressure** | Sample or pay; both are real trade-offs | None — full ingestion is the default |
| **Setup time** | Hours (SDK + project config) | Minutes |
| **Cost at 10M traces/mo** | ~$22,717/mo (Growth + overage) | $300/mo flat |
| **Cost at 100M traces/mo** | ~$178,000-$227,000/mo (Enterprise, discount range) | $1,500/mo flat |
| **Cost at 200M traces/mo** | ~$359,000-$477,000/mo (Enterprise, discount range) | $1,500/mo flat |

## Understanding your trace volume

Before evaluating platforms, you need a grip on what actually drives trace volume, because it is not a proxy for request volume — it compounds with agent complexity.

**Trace volume drivers:**
- **Request count.** Every customer request or API call that touches an LLM or agent produces at least one trace.
- **Instrumentation depth.** Each LLM call, tool call, memory read, and error handler produces its own span. A single customer request routed through a 3-step agent with two tool calls and a retry can easily produce 8-12 spans.
- **Multi-turn interactions.** Chat and session-based agents multiply trace count by conversation length, not just by session count.
- **Debugging and staging traffic.** Pre-production environments often log more verbosely than production, and that volume counts against quota too if it shares a project.
- **Retries and fallbacks.** Error paths generate additional spans even when the final response succeeds, which means your unhappy-path volume can rival your happy-path volume during a bad deploy.

**A rough formula:**

```
(requests/hour) × 24 × 30 × (traces per request) = monthly traces
```

Example: 100 requests/hour × 24 × 30 × 8 traces/request = 576,000 traces/month for a modest single-agent deployment. Multiply agents, and this number stops being modest fast — a team running 10 agents at similar request volume is already past 5M/month before anyone budgeted for it.

If you're already on LangSmith, check Settings → Usage for actual historical volume rather than estimating. That number, not a back-of-envelope guess, is the input that matters. If you want a more general walkthrough of attributing spend to specific workflows and calls rather than just totals, see [Uncoil's guide to trace-based cost optimization](/blog/ai-agent-cost-optimization) — the same instrumentation discipline that helps you cut token spend also gives you an accurate trace-volume baseline for this comparison.

**Spans per request by agent shape:**

| Agent pattern | Typical spans/request | 1M requests/month → traces/month |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Single LLM call, no tools | 1-2 | 1M-2M |
| Single agent, 1-2 tool calls | 4-6 | 4M-6M |
| Multi-step agent with retries | 8-12 | 8M-12M |
| Multi-agent orchestration (3-5 sub-agents) | 15-30 | 15M-30M |
| Multi-agent with memory + evaluation hooks | 25-45 | 25M-45M |

The jump from a single-agent pattern to a multi-agent orchestration pattern isn't a 2x increase in trace volume — it's closer to 10-20x, because each sub-agent contributes its own tool calls, memory reads, and retry paths on top of the orchestrator's own spans. Teams that budget for observability based on their current single-agent trace volume and then ship a multi-agent redesign are the ones most likely to get blindsided by an overage bill, because the volume math changed by an order of magnitude in a single release, not gradually.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Operational insight" title="Trace volume grows faster than request volume." body="As teams instrument more deeply and ship more agents, trace volume can grow 2-3x faster than request volume. If you're at 10M traces/month today but have 5 new agents queued for this quarter, plan for 30-50M traces/month within 12 months. LangSmith's overage cost compounds with that growth; Uncoil's flat tier absorbs it without a line-item change." tone="yellow" /%}

## The four volume tiers, worked in full

The rest of this article uses one consistent model so the numbers hold together end to end, rather than switching assumptions between sections. LangSmith figures assume a Growth plan (base $100/mo + $39/seat, 1M traces/month included) for teams under roughly 20M monthly traces, and a negotiated Enterprise plan (base $1,500/mo + $99/seat, 10M traces/month included, with an optional 25% committed-volume discount) above that line. Uncoil figures assume the Pro tier ($300/mo flat, generous included-events ceiling) until volume genuinely requires the Enterprise tier ($1,500/mo flat), which we call out explicitly where it applies. All figures are illustrative modeling based on public per-trace overage pricing and typical seat costs — verify current numbers against both vendors' pricing pages for a contract decision.

### Tier 1 — 10M traces/month (early production, 3 seats)

**LangSmith (Growth plan):**
- Base + seats: $100 + (3 × $39) = $217/month
- Included quota: 1,000,000 traces
- Overage: 9,000,000 traces ÷ 1,000 × $2.50 = $22,500
- **Total: $22,717/month ($272,604/year)**

**Uncoil (Pro tier):**
- Flat monthly tier, full 10M traces ingested, no quota check
- **Total: $300/month ($3,600/year)**

**Gap: ~76x.** At 10M traces/month, this is already not a close call. A team of three engineers generating 10M traces/month — which is a plausible number for a handful of moderately active agents — pays roughly $22,400/month more on LangSmith than on Uncoil for the same ingestion. Over a year that's $269,000, more than a mid-level engineer's fully loaded cost.

### Tier 2 — 30M traces/month (scaling team, 5 seats)

At this volume, most teams are past what a Growth plan comfortably covers and are negotiating an Enterprise agreement.

**LangSmith (Enterprise plan, no discount):**
- Base + seats: $1,500 + (5 × $99) = $1,995/month
- Included quota: 10,000,000 traces
- Overage: 20,000,000 traces ÷ 1,000 × $2.50 = $50,000
- **Total: $51,995/month ($623,940/year)**

**Uncoil (Pro tier):**
- Still comfortably within Pro's included-events ceiling
- **Total: $300/month ($3,600/year)**

**Gap: ~173x.** This is the tier where the "costs stay close at moderate volume" story that many comparison posts repeat simply stops being true. Unless your monthly volume is genuinely under a couple million traces, the crossover already happened.

### Tier 3 — 100M traces/month (production scale, 8 seats)

**LangSmith (Enterprise plan):**
- Base + seats: $1,500 + (8 × $99) = $2,292/month
- Included quota: 10,000,000 traces
- Overage (list rate): 90,000,000 ÷ 1,000 × $2.50 = $225,000
- Overage (25% committed-volume discount): $225,000 × 0.75 = $168,750
- **Total: $227,292/month (list) or $171,042/month (discounted)**
- **Annualized: $2,727,504 (list) or $2,052,504 (discounted)**

**Uncoil (Enterprise tier):**
- At this ingestion rate — roughly 800M-1B span-level events once you account for 8-10 spans per trace — Uncoil's own model steps up from Pro to the Enterprise tier
- **Total: $1,500/month ($18,000/year)**

**Gap: ~114-152x.** Note the shape here: Uncoil's own multiple actually looks *smaller* than Tier 2's 173x, purely because Uncoil itself moved up a tier between 30M and 100M traces. That's an honest artifact of Uncoil's step pricing, not a data error — the absolute dollar gap ($169,500-$225,800/month) is far larger than Tier 2's, even though the ratio dipped slightly.

### Tier 4 — 200M traces/month (large-scale, 10 seats)

**LangSmith (Enterprise plan):**
- Base + seats: $1,500 + (10 × $99) = $2,490/month
- Included quota: 10,000,000 traces
- Overage (list rate): 190,000,000 ÷ 1,000 × $2.50 = $475,000
- Overage (25% committed-volume discount): $475,000 × 0.75 = $356,250
- **Total: $477,490/month (list) or $358,740/month (discounted)**
- **Annualized: $5,729,880 (list) or $4,304,880 (discounted)**

**Uncoil (Enterprise tier):**
- **Total: $1,500/month ($18,000/year)**

**Gap: ~239-318x.** Even with the most aggressive discount a committed-volume Enterprise contract is likely to secure, LangSmith's bill is roughly a quarter of a million dollars higher per month than Uncoil's, for identical ingestion.

![Architecture diagram comparing LangSmith's LangChain-centric pipeline, which routes every trace through a quota gate that bills overage at $2.50 per 1,000, against Uncoil's pipeline of SDK to Inngest to Tinybird, Convex, and Redis, which has no quota gate at any point](/media/langsmith-vs-uncoil-architecture.svg)

### Why the gap isn't linear across tiers

Looking at the four multiples together — 76x, 173x, 114-152x, 239-318x — the pattern is not a smooth curve, and that's worth explaining rather than smoothing over. Two things move independently: LangSmith's overage bill scales almost linearly with volume once you're past quota, while Uncoil's price is a step function that only changes at large jumps in tier. The ratio between a linear line and a step function will always look uneven at the boundaries. What doesn't move is the trend: the absolute dollar gap grows at every tier, from roughly $22,400/month at Tier 1 to roughly $357,000-$476,000/month at Tier 4. The ratio is a useful headline number; the dollar gap is the number finance actually budgets against.

### Hidden costs the invoice doesn't show

The monthly bill is the most visible number, but it isn't the only cost a quota-based model imposes. Three costs sit underneath the invoice and rarely show up in a vendor comparison, because they're paid in engineering time and incident response rather than a line item.

| Hidden cost | How it shows up under a quota model | How it shows up under flat pricing |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Sampling engineering** | Someone designs, builds, and maintains a sampling strategy (always capture errors, sample the rest) — typically 1-3 weeks of senior engineering time up front, plus ongoing tuning as traffic patterns shift | Not needed; full ingestion is the default behavior |
| **Incident diagnosis time** | On-call engineers spend longer on root-causing production issues because the specific trace they need may have been sampled out, forcing reconstruction from logs or metrics instead | Root-cause time is bounded by how fast you can query, not by whether the trace exists at all |
| **Budget renegotiation cycles** | Finance and engineering re-open the LangSmith contract every time volume approaches the next quota boundary, which for a fast-growing team can mean quarterly renegotiation | The plan tier changes only at large, predictable jumps — renegotiation is rare |

None of these costs are hypothetical. A sampling strategy that takes two senior engineer-weeks to build and one engineer-day per month to maintain is a real, ongoing cost that never appears on the LangSmith invoice, but it exists precisely because the invoice would otherwise be unaffordable. Multiply that by a fully loaded senior engineer rate and the "savings" from sampling look considerably smaller than the sticker price suggests.

## Scenario: a team's actual first year

Static tiers are useful for comparison, but most teams don't sit still at one volume — they grow into it. Here's a plausible trajectory for a team that starts with a modest production footprint and scales three agents into ten over a year, using the same Growth-to-Enterprise transition modeled above.

| Milestone | Monthly traces | Seats | LangSmith monthly bill | Uncoil monthly bill |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Day 0 | 5M | 3 | $10,217 | $300 |
| Month 3 | 25M | 5 | $39,495 | $300 |
| Month 6 | 60M | 7 | $127,193 | $300 |
| Month 12 | 120M | 10 | $277,490 | $1,500 |

Holding each rate constant for the stretch until the next milestone (a stepped approximation, not a precise integral), the first year's *actual* cumulative spend looks like this:

- **LangSmith:** (3 months × $10,217) + (3 months × $39,495) + (6 months × $127,193) = $30,651 + $118,485 + $763,158 = **$912,294**
- **Uncoil:** roughly 9 months on Pro at $300 + 3 months on Enterprise at $1,500 = $2,700 + $4,500 = **$7,200**

That's a **~127x gap in year-one actual spend** — before the Month 12 exit run-rate of $277,490/month (LangSmith) versus $1,500/month (Uncoil) even reflects in the following year's budget. Teams that hit this trajectory and stay on LangSmith are often not choosing to pay $912K for observability; they're discovering it a quarter at a time, after the contract is already signed and the SDK is already wired into a hundred call sites.

> **Decision point:** if you expect 2-3x trace volume growth within 12 months — a new product line, a wave of new agents, a customer segment that's about to onboard — model your Month 12 number before you sign, not after you see the invoice.

## Where LangSmith genuinely wins

None of the math above is an argument that Uncoil wins on every dimension. It wins on cost at any meaningful production volume — that's the point of this article — but cost is not the only axis a platform decision should turn on, and pretending otherwise would make this comparison less useful, not more.

**LangChain and LangGraph integration.** If your stack is built on LangChain or LangGraph, LangSmith's tracing is close to automatic. Chains, agents, and tool calls are instrumented with minimal added code, and the trace hierarchy maps directly onto your LangChain constructs. This is a real, unhedged advantage for LangChain-native teams, and it's the single strongest reason to stay on LangSmith even at moderate cost premiums.

**Prompt versioning and the playground.** LangSmith's prompt hub ties versioned prompts directly to the traces they produced, and its playground for iterating on prompts against real trace inputs is mature. Teams doing heavy prompt-engineering iteration will feel the difference immediately.

**Developer experience on the trace inspector.** The LangSmith UI for browsing individual traces — request/response pairs, chain hierarchies, latency waterfalls — is polished and has had years to mature. This is a legitimate strength independent of price.

**On-premises and data residency options.** LangSmith Cloud is the default, but Enterprise customers can negotiate self-hosted or VPC deployments. For teams with strict data residency requirements, air-gapped environments, or regulatory constraints that rule out third-party SaaS for raw trace data, this option matters more than any cost delta. Uncoil is SaaS-only today.

**SDK-level sampling control.** LangSmith lets you decide, in code, exactly which traces to send and at what fidelity. For teams that have already built a deliberate, well-tested sampling strategy — not a cost-driven afterthought, but an intentional architecture — that flexibility is a real capability, not just a cost-avoidance workaround.

**Maturity and community.** LangSmith has been in production longer than most competing platforms, which shows up in less obvious ways: more Stack Overflow answers, more community-contributed integration snippets, a support team that has already seen your specific edge case, and documentation that covers years of accumulated corner cases. For a team hitting an unusual integration problem at 2 AM, that maturity has real value that doesn't show up in a feature comparison table.

**Evaluation-as-a-workflow, not just a feature.** LangSmith's evaluation tooling grew up alongside its trace inspector, so moving from "I see a bad trace" to "I've built a regression test from it" tends to feel like a continuous workflow rather than a context switch, particularly for teams already living inside the LangChain ecosystem.

> **Decision point:** if your team is LangChain-native, iterates heavily on prompts, and stays under roughly 10-20M traces/month with no near-term growth plan past that, LangSmith's developer experience and ecosystem fit may reasonably outweigh Uncoil's cost advantage. This isn't a universal recommendation — it's a real trade-off with a real answer for some teams.

## Why the pricing structure shapes more than the invoice

The choice between quota-based and flat pricing changes how your team behaves, not just what you pay. Three effects compound over time.

### The sampling trap

When trace volume exceeds quota, the instinctive response is to sample — capture a representative subset and stay under budget. The intention is reasonable. The result usually isn't, because sampling removes exactly the traces you need most:

- **Rare failures.** A bug affecting 1% of requests is statistically invisible in a 10% random sample.
- **Cost anomalies.** The one customer session that burned $50 in LLM spend is exactly as likely to be dropped as any other trace.
- **Cascading errors.** A failure that only manifests after 50 sequential tool calls rarely survives random sampling intact.
- **Incident response.** When production breaks, the traces you need to diagnose it are, by definition, the tail — and the tail is what sampling cuts first.
- **Model drift on specific input shapes.** If a provider's model update degrades quality only for long-context requests, that degradation is invisible in a sample that doesn't preferentially capture long-context traces.

Sophisticated teams build smarter sampling — always capture errors, always capture high-cost requests, sample only the boring middle. That's a legitimate engineering response, but it's also weeks of engineering time spent solving a pricing problem instead of a product problem. For a deeper look at the broader discipline of separating genuine cost control from accidental quality loss, see [Uncoil's guide to reducing LLM costs without hurting quality](/blog/reduce-llm-costs-without-hurting-quality) — the same principle that applies to token spend applies to trace sampling: cuts that are invisible until they cost you an incident are not free.

### Cost predictability and the viral-traffic problem

LangSmith's bill is a function of actual usage, which means it moves with your traffic — including traffic spikes you didn't plan for. If your product has a viral moment and traffic jumps 5x for a week, your trace volume jumps roughly 5x too, and your overage bill jumps with it, in the same billing cycle, with no warning. Teams in this position often do the worst possible thing at the worst possible time: they disable or throttle tracing in production to control the bill, right when the traffic spike is most likely to also produce the failures they need to see.

Uncoil's flat tier doesn't move. Whether you ingest 10M or 500M traces in a given month, the invoice is the same number finance already approved. The upside during a traffic spike is exactly backwards from LangSmith's: you have full observability precisely when you need it most, with zero billing risk.

{% highlight %}The worst time to lose observability is the same moment a pricing model built on per-trace overage gives teams the strongest incentive to turn it off — the week traffic spikes and the bill spikes with it.{% /highlight %}

### Architectural freedom

When trace budget is scarce, engineers start asking "how much quota do I have left" before they ask "what information would help me debug this." That's a subtle but real shift in incentive, and it compounds: instrumentation gets sparser, debugging gets harder, and the platform that was supposed to make production visible ends up making it more opaque than an unconstrained team would tolerate. Flat pricing removes that incentive entirely — the question reverts to what it should have been: what do you need to see.

{% editorial-panel eyebrow="Field note" title="A $2M line item rarely announces itself in advance." body="Teams that end up with a seven-figure LangSmith bill almost never decided to spend that much on observability. They signed a Growth-tier contract at 2M traces/month, shipped six more agents over the following year, and only noticed the trajectory when finance flagged the invoice. Running the Tier 3 and Tier 4 math above against your own 12-month roadmap — before you sign, not after — is the entire difference." tone="dark" /%}

## Decision framework

![Flowchart branching by monthly trace volume: under 1M traces either platform works, 1M to 10M traces overage begins to bite and Uncoil is the safer default, 10M to 50M and 50M-plus traces both recommend Uncoil decisively unless on-premises deployment is required, in which case LangSmith's negotiated on-prem option is the exception](/media/langsmith-vs-uncoil-decision-flow.svg)

| Decision factor | LangSmith advantage | Uncoil advantage |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Volume &lt; 1M/mo** | Cost parity, mature UI | Flat cost, no quota to track |
| **Volume 1-10M/mo** | Ecosystem fit if LangChain-native | 76x+ cost gap already visible |
| **Volume 10-50M/mo** | Case-by-case Enterprise support | 150-200x cost gap, no sampling forced |
| **Volume &gt; 50M/mo** | Negotiated on-prem, if required | 100-300x cost gap, full fidelity at fixed cost |
| **LangChain / LangGraph stack** | Native, near-automatic instrumentation | Explicit instrumentation, framework-agnostic |
| **Prompt versioning + playground** | Mature, tightly integrated | Supported via evaluation context, less specialized |
| **Data residency / on-prem** | Negotiable for Enterprise | SaaS only |
| **Integrated evaluation** | Available, often a separate workflow | Native, in the same dashboard as traces |
| **Cost breakdown by model/provider/agent** | Requires manual work or a separate tool | Built into the platform by default |
| **Budget predictability during growth** | Bill moves with traffic | Bill is fixed regardless of traffic |

**Choose LangSmith when:** you're prototyping under roughly 1M traces/month; your stack is LangChain or LangGraph-native and instrumentation simplicity matters more than the cost delta; you have a hard data-residency or air-gapped requirement that only a negotiated on-prem deployment satisfies; or your prompt-iteration workflow depends on the playground and prompt hub specifically.

**Choose Uncoil when:** your production volume is already past a few million traces/month; you need cost breakdown by model, provider, or agent without building it yourself; you want one flat number in the budget regardless of how traffic behaves; you're planning to add agents or scale usage over the next 12 months; or you're currently sampling on LangSmith and know it — which is itself a sign the quota stopped fitting the workload a while ago.

## Migration guidance

If the math above points toward switching, the migration itself is more mechanical than risky, but it benefits from sequencing.

**1. Run both platforms in parallel first.** Don't cut over in one step. Instrument new services on Uncoil while LangSmith continues running on existing ones, and compare trace fidelity and dashboard output for at least one full sprint before decommissioning anything.

**2. Instrument at the OpenTelemetry layer where possible.** Both platforms speak OTel-compatible spans in some form; centralizing instrumentation at that layer rather than calling vendor SDKs directly throughout your codebase makes any future switch — in either direction — a configuration change instead of a rewrite.

**3. Decide what historical data actually needs to move.** Most teams don't migrate historical traces wholesale; they let LangSmith's existing data age out under its own retention policy while new data flows to the new platform, and export only the specific evaluation datasets or golden traces they need for regression testing.

**4. Rebuild alerts and dashboards before cutover, not after.** Any alert rules, saved views, or dashboards built against LangSmith's schema need an equivalent on the new platform before you flip traffic — an observability gap during a migration is exactly the wrong week to have one.

**5. Keep LangSmith read access for a defined window.** A 30-60 day overlap where LangSmith stays queryable (even if no new traces are landing there) covers the edge case of needing to look something up from before the cutover.

**6. Re-run the volume math quarterly, not once.** The tier boundaries in this article aren't static for your account — they move as your product grows. A decision that was correct at 8M traces/month can be stale at 40M.

> **Decision rule:** Pick LangSmith when LangChain-native development workflow is the constraint; pick Uncoil when cross-framework production diagnosis and cost accountability are the constraint.

## Action checklist

- [ ] Pull your actual monthly trace volume from LangSmith Settings → Usage, or calculate it with the formula above if you're not yet instrumented.
- [ ] Match your volume to one of the four tiers above and read the corresponding math against your actual seat count.
- [ ] Project 12-month growth realistically: 20-50% for stable products, 50-200% for growing ones, 200%+ if you're shipping new agents or entering new markets.
- [ ] Check whether you're currently sampling, throttling tracing, or shortening retention to control cost — each of those is a sign the quota already stopped fitting.
- [ ] Weigh the non-cost factors honestly: LangChain fit, prompt-versioning needs, on-prem or data-residency requirements.
- [ ] If switching, plan the parallel-run window, alert rebuild, and historical-data decision before touching the cutover date.
- [ ] Bring finance and procurement into the decision with the actual 12-month number, not the current month's invoice.

## Frequently asked questions

{% faq-item question="At what trace volume do LangSmith overages become the deciding factor?" answer="Based on the Growth-plan quota assumption used throughout this article (1M included traces/month), overage costs already exceed the base plan cost well under 5M traces/month, and by 10M/month the gap versus a flat-rate platform is already roughly 76x. There isn't a volume where overage is negligible once you're meaningfully past the included quota — the crossover happens earlier than most teams expect." /%}

{% faq-item question="Can we negotiate better overage rates with LangSmith?" answer="Enterprise customers can typically negotiate volume discounts, modeled here at roughly 20-30% off the standard $2.50/1K overage rate for committed-volume contracts. Even at a 25% discount, the Tier 4 math above still shows a 239x gap versus Uncoil's flat Enterprise tier — negotiation narrows the multiple, it doesn't close it." /%}

{% faq-item question="Is sampling a reasonable way to control LangSmith costs?" answer="It controls cost, but it degrades observability in ways that are easy to underestimate: rare failures, cost anomalies, and long error chains are exactly the traces random sampling is most likely to drop. Before sampling to hit a budget, quantify what you'd lose — if the answer is 'the traces we'd need during an incident,' the savings are smaller than they look on the invoice." /%}

{% faq-item question="Does Uncoil have its own overage fees at very high volume?" answer="Uncoil's pricing is tier-based rather than per-trace: the Pro tier covers a generous included-events ceiling, and volume beyond that moves you to the Enterprise tier at a flat $1,500/month rather than triggering a per-trace meter. Extremely large deployments (500M+ traces/month) should confirm current tier boundaries directly with Uncoil, but there is no per-trace overage line item at any tier." /%}

{% faq-item question="If we use LangSmith for prompt development and Uncoil for production, is that a reasonable split?" answer="It's workable but not free: you maintain two SDK integrations, train the team on two UIs, and lose the single-pane cost visibility that comes from one platform seeing both prompt iteration and production traffic. Some teams do run this split deliberately — prompt hub in LangSmith, production tracing in Uncoil — but it should be a considered trade-off, not a default, since the operational overhead is real and ongoing." /%}
